POSNETEK_A = REC(
    "A chocolate maker",
    "You will listen to an interview with Anna Dupont, who runs a small chocolate shop in York.",
    """Interviewer: Anna, your shop is famous for its handmade chocolates. Have you always loved chocolate?

Anna: Always! My father is Belgian, and every Christmas we visited my grandparents in Bruges. My grandmother would take me to a little chocolate shop near the market square, and I was allowed to choose one chocolate from every tray. I must have been about six. I remember thinking that the man behind the counter had the best job in the world.

Interviewer: But you didn't start making chocolate straight away.

Anna: No, I trained as a nurse and worked in a children's hospital for eight years. I loved the job, but it was exhausting, and I was making chocolates at home in the evenings to relax. Friends started ordering them for birthdays and weddings, and one day I realised I was spending more time thinking about chocolate than about anything else.

Interviewer: So you gave up nursing?

Anna: Not immediately. First I did a six-month course in Belgium, in the same town where I'd been as a child. My teacher was very strict – I spent the first two weeks just learning how to melt chocolate properly. If you get the temperature wrong, the chocolate goes grey and loses its shine.

Interviewer: What makes a good chocolate?

Anna: Good ingredients, first of all. I buy my cocoa beans directly from two small farms, one in Ecuador and one in Ghana, so I know that the farmers are paid fairly. Then patience. Some of my chocolates take three days to make. And imagination – I like unusual flavours. My best-seller is chocolate with sea salt and Yorkshire honey.

Interviewer: Are there any flavours that didn't work?

Anna: Oh, plenty! Chocolate with cheese was a disaster. And I once made a batch with chilli that was so hot that a customer had to drink three glasses of water. I put a warning on the box after that.

Interviewer: What's the hardest part of running the shop?

Anna: The summer. Chocolate melts at around thirty degrees, so in hot weather we have to keep everything in special cabinets, and we can't send orders by post. Christmas and Easter are the busiest times – at Easter we make over four thousand chocolate eggs, and I hardly sleep for two weeks.

Interviewer: Do you ever get tired of chocolate?

Anna: Never. I taste everything I make, of course, but only a tiny piece. People are always surprised that I'm not fat! The secret is that once you work with chocolate every day, you don't want to eat a whole box.

Interviewer: What are your plans for the future?

Anna: I'd like to run chocolate-making workshops for children. Many of them have no idea that chocolate comes from a bean that grows on a tree. When they find out, their faces light up – just like mine did in Bruges.""")

POSNETEK_B = REC(
    "Living on a narrowboat",
    "You will listen to a conversation with Jess and Sam Turner, who live on a narrowboat on the canals of England.",
    """Presenter: Jess and Sam, you live on a boat. Why did you decide to leave dry land?

Jess: Mainly because of money. We were living in a small flat in Birmingham, and the rent took more than half of our salaries. A friend of ours lived on a boat, and when we visited her, we fell in love with the idea. Two years later, we sold almost everything we owned and bought a second-hand narrowboat.

Presenter: What exactly is a narrowboat?

Sam: It's a long, thin boat designed for the canals, which were built in the eighteenth century to transport goods like coal. Our boat is eighteen metres long but only two metres wide, so it's a bit like living in a corridor. We have a tiny kitchen, a bathroom, a bedroom and a living area with a wood-burning stove.

Presenter: Isn't it very small for two people?

Jess: It is, and at first we argued a lot about space. We had to learn to be very tidy – everything has its place. But it's amazing how little you really need. We have one shelf of books each and about a third of the clothes we used to have.

Presenter: Do you stay in one place?

Sam: No. We don't have a permanent mooring, so the rules say we have to move to a new place at least every fourteen days. That's one of the things we love most – we've lived in the middle of cities and in the deep countryside, sometimes with no other boats in sight.

Presenter: What about practical things like water and electricity?

Jess: We have solar panels on the roof, which give us enough electricity in summer, but in winter we sometimes have to run the engine to charge the batteries. Water comes from taps along the canal, and we fill our tank about once a week. You quickly learn not to waste it. Showers are very short!

Presenter: What's the hardest part?

Sam: Winter, definitely. The boat is made of steel, so it gets very cold, and sometimes the canal freezes and we can't move. One January we were stuck for three weeks. We had to carry our shopping along the towpath for two kilometres.

Jess: And it can be quite isolated. Our families were worried at first that we'd be lonely. But there's a strong community among boat people. Everyone helps each other – if your engine breaks down, someone always stops to help.

Presenter: Would you ever go back to living in a house?

Jess: Maybe one day, when we have children. But not yet.

Sam: I'd miss waking up and seeing a heron outside the window.""")

